@@ -0,0 +1,57 @@
+# Design: Move off left-pad
+
+Ecosystem: JavaScript / TypeScript. Current version in use: latest is 1.3.0.
+
+## Approach
+
+Replace calls to `left-pad` with `String.prototype.padStart`. No package is added to the product. Successor kind: native.
+
+The project owns one small module, `src/pad.js`, exporting `padLeft(value, width, fill)`. It calls `String(value).padStart(width, pad)` and decides `pad` (see "Known behavioural differences"). It has no dependency. The call sites in `src/receipt.js` switch to it:
+
+- `leftPad(order.id, 8, '0')` becomes `padLeft(order.id, 8, '0')`
+- `leftPad('x' + item.qty, QTY_WIDTH)` becomes `padLeft('x' + item.qty, QTY_WIDTH)`
+- `leftPad(money(...), PRICE_WIDTH, fill)` (two places) becomes `padLeft(money(...), PRICE_WIDTH, fill)`
+
+A bare `padStart` at each call site would change the output for the free `fill` option, as the séance showed (next section). `padLeft` keeps the old behaviour wherever the old behaviour was sensible.
+
+## The séance (differential test)
+
+File: `test/seance.leftpad.test.js`. Runner: `node:test` (the project's runner), with `fast-check` called through `fc.assert` inside the test. The test name is `séance: left-pad vs String.prototype.padStart`.
+
+For each call-site shape the test:
+
+1. generates inputs from the real domain: value is a number (negative, zero, fractional, huge, `NaN`) or a string (empty, long, unicode); width is small, zero, negative or large; `fill` is an arbitrary short string including `''`, single characters and multi-character strings;
+2. calls `left-pad` and `String.prototype.padStart` with the same input;
+3. fails if the results differ, or if exactly one of them throws, and shows the shrunk input and both outputs.
+
+Old versus old (`left-pad` on both sides) is run first to prove the harness.
+
+First run of the séance with a bare `String(value).padStart(width, fill)` as the new side failed (shrunk counterexamples):
+
+- `value "0.00", width 10, fill ""`: left-pad `"      0.00"`, padStart `"0.00"`
+- `value "", width 1, fill ".."`: left-pad `".."`, padStart `"."`
+
+Further probing found `fill` null and false (left-pad pads with spaces, padStart writes `"nulln$6.00"` and `"false$6.00"`) and an astral fill such as `"👻"` (left-pad writes whole ghosts, padStart cuts a surrogate pair in half).
+
+## Risks
+
+- A behaviour that the inventory missed. The test only covers the APIs it lists.
+- Differences the project relies on by accident (error messages, key order). Decide per case whether to match or to change the caller.
+- `fill` is a free user option, so differences in how `left-pad` and `padStart` treat unusual fills reach users.
+
+## Known behavioural differences
+
+Decision, taken from the séance's findings: match the old behaviour where it was sensible, accept the padStart behaviour where the old behaviour was a defect.
+
+| Input (`fill` is the `fill` option) | left-pad | padStart | Decision |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `fill` is `''`, `null`, `false` or `NaN` (falsy, other than the number 0) | pads with spaces | `''` adds nothing; `null` and `false` are written as the words `null` and `false` | **Matched.** `padLeft` pads with spaces, as before. A blank fill therefore still aligns the money column. |
+| `fill` is a number such as `0` or `5` | uses it as a string | uses it as a string | Same. |
+| `fill` longer than one character, value shorter than `width` (`"ab"`, `".."`, `"👻"`) | writes the whole fill once per missing column, so the line is longer than `width` (`leftPad('$6.00', 10, 'ab')` is `"ababababab$6.00"`, 15 characters) | repeats the fill and cuts it at `width` (`"ababa$6.00"`, 10 characters) | **Accepted.** The padStart result keeps every line at `width`, which is what the receipt's "every line of the table has the same width" test expects. The séance asserts this contract for these inputs and asserts that left-pad really overshoots, so the exception cannot go stale. |
+| `fill` is an astral character such as `"👻"` (two UTF-16 units), odd number of columns to fill | writes whole characters but overshoots `width` | cuts the character in half and leaves a lone surrogate | **Accepted, not fixed.** Same cause as the row above. Use a fill made of one UTF-16 unit, or an even padding width, to avoid it. Recorded as a risk for the owner of the `fill` option. |
+
+Nothing else differed over the generated inputs: numbers and strings as the value, widths from -5 to 5000, string fills including `''`, single characters and multi-character strings, and non-string fills.
+
+## Order of work
+
+Inventory, then the séance against the old code, then the switch, then the removal.
